{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC comment/description length convergence — and settle whether the comment limit is even right\n\n## Context\n\nCloud has two convergence suites that exist because oversized text caused real incidents:\n`mutate/test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py` and\n`mutate/test_outbound_description_length_convergence.py`. Each drives an oversized value through\nTWO passes and asserts the second plans nothing — i.e. the truncated value the adapter sent must\ncompare equal to what it reads back, or the differ re-emits the change forever.\n\n**DC has neither.** And DC's comment path is the one with a question mark over it.\n\n## The specific doubt, verified in source\n\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:146-149`:\n\n    def sanitize_comment(self, body: str) -> str:\n        return _shared_sanitize_comment(\n            body, truncate=self._codec.fit_outbound, max_chars=WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT\n        )\n\nTwo things are bound to the DESCRIPTION here: the limit (`WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT`, 32767) and the\nfitter (`fit_outbound`, the description fit). Cloud does not do this — it routes comments through a\ndedicated `adapters/jira/comment_limits` module.\n\n**RESOLVED — and the original suspicion here was WRONG, so this section is corrected rather than\nleft standing.** This story was written believing that whether 32767 is the correct DC comment\nceiling was UNKNOWN, and that Cloud's separate `comment_limits` module existed \"precisely because\ncomment limits are not description limits\". Both are now settled against primary sources:\n\n* 32767 IS the correct DC comment ceiling for stock Jira >= 7.0 — it is the default of\n  `jira.text.field.character.limit`, whose own definition covers \"Description, Environment,\n  **Comments** and Text custom fields\". Comments and text custom fields are ONE property, so DC\n  binding both to one constant is faithful to DC, not a shortcut.\n* Cloud's `comment_limits` module exists because the UNITS differ, NOT the numbers: Cloud comments\n  are limited in PLAIN characters (32767) while Cloud descriptions are limited in ADF-SERIALIZED\n  size (`_ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` = 32000, fitted empirically because `text_to_adf` inflates — a\n  32767-char description was measured serializing to ~50,566 ADF chars). DC has no ADF inflation,\n  which is why one constant genuinely suffices there.\n\nSo the substance of this story is NOT \"find out, then pin it\". It is: add the two DC convergence\nsuites DC lacks, and cite the ceiling's provenance at the binding so the constant stops reading as\nan assumption.\n\n## Approach\n\n1. THE CEILING IS ALREADY ESTABLISHED — do not re-measure it. Jira's own `jpm.xml` defines\n   `jira.text.field.character.limit` with `default-value 32767` and a description covering\n   \"Description, Environment, **Comments** and Text custom fields\", and JRASERVER-28519 records\n   that 7.0.0 made 32767 the default. Comments and text custom fields are ONE property by design,\n   so binding DC's comment limit to the same constant as its description limit is faithful to DC.\n   The task is therefore to CITE that provenance in the source at the binding, so the constant\n   reads as a decision rather than an assumption. Full evidence is in the research comment on this\n   ticket. (That the limit is admin-configurable, and so wrong on a reconfigured instance, is a\n   real but separate defect tracked as [rebar:049e-9fac-a821-4ea2]; it does NOT gate this story.)\n2. Add the DC convergence suites as **SEPARATE FILES** in `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/`.\n   DO NOT parametrize them alongside Cloud's. Cloud's two suites hardcode Cloud-only internals\n   that have no DC equivalent — `comment_limits.truncate_comment_body`\n   (`test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py:154`), and `ADF_PATH` /\n   `_ADF_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT = 32000` (`test_outbound_description_length_convergence.py:31-34`) —\n   whereas DC fits via `WikiTextCodec.fit_outbound`, a plain character truncation at\n   `WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT`, with no ADF serialization step at all. Parametrizing would require\n   restructuring those incident-hardened Cloud tests, which this epic's own acceptance criteria\n   forbid (\"Cloud's pre-existing assertions still pass UNEDITED\"). This mirrors the split that\n   `cedc-58d1-f6d1-428e` names as its fallback; here the split is the DEFAULT, not the fallback,\n   because the blocker is already known rather than something to discover by trying.\n3. Only then decide whether `max_chars` needs changing. A limit change is a BEHAVIOUR change and,\n   if needed, belongs in its own bug ticket linked from here — not folded into a test story.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/mutate/` — NEW, DC-specific convergence files for comment and\n  description. Cloud's two existing convergence files must be left BYTE-UNCHANGED.\n- the `max_chars=WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` binding in `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py` — a\n  provenance COMMENT only, citing `jira.text.field.character.limit` and JRASERVER-28519.\n- NOT the limit change itself, and NOT making the limit configurable (that is\n  [rebar:049e-9fac-a821-4ea2]).\n\n## Testing\n\nConvergence is a two-pass property, so a single-pass assertion cannot establish it: the test must\nrun the pass twice and assert the SECOND plans nothing for that ticket. A first-pass-only test\nwould pass against a bridge that re-sends the same truncated comment forever.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] an oversized DC comment converges: pass 2 plans no further change for that ticket, asserted\n      on the plan rather than on the absence of an exception\n- [ ] an oversized DC description converges, same assertion shape\n- [ ] the truncation suffix marker survives the round trip and is asserted, not just the length\n- [ ] the `max_chars=WIKI_DESCRIPTION_LIMIT` binding carries a source comment citing\n      `jira.text.field.character.limit` and JRASERVER-28519, so a reader cannot mistake the 32767\n      for an assumption — verified by reading the comment in the diff\n- [ ] Cloud's two existing convergence files are BYTE-UNCHANGED (`git diff` shows no edit to\n      `test_outbound_comment_length_convergence.py` or\n      `test_outbound_description_length_convergence.py`), because the DC suites are separate files\n- [ ] the limit itself is NOT changed and is NOT made configurable by this story; that work stays\n      in [rebar:049e-9fac-a821-4ea2]\n- [ ] mutation-checked: raising the limit above the real ceiling, or removing the fit, turns a\n      convergence test red"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785699910045116001,"uuid":"f8a47e1e-3319-411d-96f2-f7bd8cd332b3"}